---
// Lesson 8.4 — Boss Attacks: Patterns & Phases
// Telegraphed attacks: shockwave + charge; phase tables drive behavior.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>8.4 — Boss Attacks: Patterns &amp; Phases</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The boss can be hurt (<a href="8.3.html">8.3</a>) but does not
    fight back. Today you give it the two attacks your spec promised
    — a shockwave the player jumps and a charge the player sidesteps
    — both TELEGRAPHED, and both reading their numbers from a phase
    table so Phase 3 is a one-line change, not a rewrite.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Attack 1 — the shockwave (you have the parts).</strong>
    It is a slow projectile fired along the ground: reuse
    <a href="3.2.html">3.2</a>'s Projectile skeleton — a trigger
    collider moving by Translate — but flat and wide:</p>
    <pre><code>public class Shockwave : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float speed = 3f;
    [SerializeField] private int damage = 1;
    [SerializeField] private float lifetime = 4f;

    private Vector2 direction;

    public void Initialize(Vector2 dir)
    {
        direction = dir.normalized;
    }

    private void Start()
    {
        Destroy(gameObject, lifetime);
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        transform.Translate(direction * speed * Time.deltaTime);
    }

    private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
    {
        if (other.CompareTag("Player"))
        {
            if (StatManager.manager != null)
                StatManager.manager.ChangeHealth(-damage);
            Destroy(gameObject);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>Why speed 3 (the turret's bullet is 10): a shockwave is a
    RHYTHM — slow enough to jump over ON PURPOSE. The wave is one
    tile tall; your jump clears two. That is the counterplay
    sentence from <a href="8.1.html">8.1</a> made real.</p>

    <p><strong>Attack 2 — the charge.</strong> The boss's own body:
    telegraph 0.6s (it rears back — scale or color flash), then
    dashes horizontally at speed 8 for 0.8s, then recovers 1.5s
    (vulnerable — your reward window). BossManager grows a tiny
    attack scheduler in the starter's voice:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private GameObject shockwavePrefab;
[SerializeField] private Transform shockwaveSpawn;
[SerializeField] private float attackInterval = 3f;
private float lastAttackTime;
private int currentPhase = 1;

private void Update()
{
    if (Time.time &gt;= lastAttackTime + attackInterval &amp;&amp; currentPhase &gt; 1)
    {
        lastAttackTime = Time.time;
        FireShockwave();
        // charge is a coroutine or a state — studio choice
    }
}

private void FireShockwave()
{
    Vector2 dir = (playerTransform.position - transform.position).x &gt; 0
        ? Vector2.right : Vector2.left;          // toward the player
    GameObject wave = Instantiate(shockwavePrefab,
        shockwaveSpawn.position, Quaternion.identity);
    wave.GetComponent&lt;Shockwave&gt;().Initialize(dir);
}</code></pre>
    <p>Read the ternary — one line picks a direction by comparing x
    positions. Same pursuit math as the chase
    (<a href="4.3.html">4.3</a>), flattened to one axis.</p>

    <p><strong>The phase table.</strong> Attack numbers belong to
    PHASES, not to one set of serialized fields. Pattern: a small
    private table and one reader —</p>
    <pre><code>// phase: 1    2    3
// cooldown: 5s   3s   1.8s
// charge speed:  6    8    10

private void EnterPhase(int phase)
{
    currentPhase = phase;
    switch (phase)
    {
        case 2: attackInterval = 3f; chargeSpeed = 8f; break;
        case 3: attackInterval = 1.8f; chargeSpeed = 10f; break;
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>Now Phase 3 is DIFFERENT because numbers changed, and the
    player SEES the difference without a tutorial. That is the whole
    trick of difficulty in fighting games.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The unreadable boss</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Set attackInterval to 0.5 in Phase 3. What happens
                to the jump rhythm — and what does "the player's
                counterplay must fit inside the attack interval"
                mean numerically for your wave speed?</li>
            <li>A shockwave spawns INSIDE the boss's collider and
                immediately touches the boss. Why does nothing
                happen (what tag does the boss carry vs the
                player)? Now spawn it inside the PLAYER by
                accident — what happens instead, and how does the
                <a href="3.2.html">3.2</a> spawn-offset fix
                translate?</li>
            <li>The charge goes through walls (a physics-layer
                accident). What should stop a charge — and is a
                wall-stop a BUG or a FEATURE (the classic
                "stunned boss" gift)?</li>
            <li>Phase 3 activates while a shockwave is mid-flight.
                Does the OLD wave speed up retroactively? Why do
                instances keep their birth numbers — and when is
                that a problem (imagine a 20-second lifetime
                wave)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Attack rhythm</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Play your own boss (spec says you must be able to
                beat it). Time the fight. If you cannot win, the
                boss is overtuned — a boss the designer cannot
                beat is not hard, it is broken.</li>
            <li>Add a third attack ONLY as a spec sentence first
                ("arena hazard spikes rise on a timer"). Do not
                code it — why does a written attack cost nothing
                and a coded one cost a day?</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"8.4a: shockwave + charge + phase table"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Build a telegraphed shockwave reusing the projectile pattern</li>
            <li>Drive attack choice and pacing from a phase table</li>
            <li>Explain counterplay arithmetic: wave height vs. jump height</li>
            <li>Tune Phase 3 until you can beat your own boss</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/index.html">Unit 8: Boss Fight</a> — attack patterns and telegraphs</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — the boss is the machine with the most states</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="8.5.html">8.5 — Win, Lose &amp; the Death Loop</a></p>
</div>`;
